other podcast leave a review.

00:00:29.669 --> 00:00:30.255
George.

00:00:30.255 --> 00:00:36.270
George live Las Vegas, where we go
to make money on the slot machines,

00:00:36.290 --> 00:00:40.400
but George speaks Japanese,
Korean, and other languages.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:44.000
I would like to, first of all, ask
George ho what do you think what

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.120
is the best way to learn Japanese?

00:00:47.839 --> 00:00:48.300
It's a big

00:00:48.324 --> 00:00:48.955
question.

00:00:49.135 --> 00:00:52.195
Uh, but I think the first question that
I always think about when I think if

00:00:52.195 --> 00:00:56.305
someone wants to learn a language, I
think, are they interested in the country?

00:00:56.495 --> 00:00:57.835
You know, are they
interested in the culture?

00:00:57.835 --> 00:01:01.195
Is there something that fascinates them
about it that would keep driving them?

00:01:01.195 --> 00:01:03.864
Because as you know, learning
a language isn't just something

00:01:03.864 --> 00:01:05.965
you go, oh, I'm going to learn a
language and a month later you have.

00:01:06.925 --> 00:01:09.265
There's a lot of, a lot of
effort that has to go into it.

00:01:09.655 --> 00:01:10.315
I think.

00:01:10.495 --> 00:01:16.405
Um, so if, if for example, you like J pop
music, you watch, you listen to music.

00:01:16.405 --> 00:01:20.935
If you like, uh, some sort of drama
in Japanese or some sort of game

00:01:20.935 --> 00:01:22.915
that's Japanese that you could get
your hands on the Japanese version.

00:01:22.915 --> 00:01:25.885
I think a good thing to do
is kind of follow your heart.

00:01:26.825 --> 00:01:29.315
Um, but if you're talking about,
of course, if you're talking,

00:01:29.494 --> 00:01:30.605
obviously I teach Japanese, right?

00:01:30.605 --> 00:01:32.765
So I'm speaking in
general terms right now.

00:01:32.884 --> 00:01:33.035
Right.

00:01:33.035 --> 00:01:36.455
But let's say you decide,
okay, I want to learn Japanese.

00:01:36.905 --> 00:01:40.895
So in my, in my way of learning,
I did the same thing with Korean.

00:01:41.375 --> 00:01:45.035
Uh, I was in a classroom in Japan
and I went to classes in Korea

00:01:45.035 --> 00:01:47.255
actually, but you don't have to do
that nowadays because the internet.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:52.590
So much, uh, access to Korean teachers
or Japanese teachers that, you know,

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:56.100
I would say, hey, get a Japanese
teacher, find a channel on YouTube.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:59.720
Find some tools that are,
like LingQ, for example.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:04.650
Find some, tools that will help you learn
and just bust through the basic grammar.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.320
I really believe you should go through
the basic grammar as fast as you

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:09.509
can and learn the writing system.

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:10.530
That would be the step one.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:12.600
I would say a couple of things.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:17.355
First of all you have learned Japanese to
a high degree of proficiency and Korean.

00:02:17.504 --> 00:02:18.584
What was your motivation?

00:02:18.584 --> 00:02:19.994
You said get motivated by something.

00:02:19.994 --> 00:02:21.974
So what motivated you to learn.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:26.460
Well, Japanese was, I mean, it's
the same for Japanese and Korean

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:31.290
for me, but, you know, uh, the start
of Japanese was, I was 12 and my

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.570
father was a military contractor.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:35.940
His company had a contract
with the military and that led

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.370
us to be up in Japan in 1985.

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:40.230
So I was 12.

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:43.720
I was in sixth grade on
the military air base.

00:02:44.079 --> 00:02:47.484
And one of the classes that I was
required to take was Japanese.

00:02:47.484 --> 00:02:48.594
So that got me started.

00:02:49.045 --> 00:02:52.945
And I think, uh, it wasn't until maybe
about the next year after that, when

00:02:52.945 --> 00:02:56.874
I started thinking about girls and
Japanese girls to me were attractive.

00:02:56.874 --> 00:02:59.814
So I just was motivated by that initially.

00:03:00.454 --> 00:03:04.275
And then the continued motivation for
me was, uh, I started listening to

00:03:04.275 --> 00:03:08.295
J-pop, like I said, and of course I
had access to a lot of fun Japanese TV

00:03:08.295 --> 00:03:11.865
every week I was watching the Japanese
top 10 to see the artists that I love.

00:03:12.075 --> 00:03:13.335
I learned the lyrics of the music.

00:03:13.335 --> 00:03:14.295
I joined the fan club.

00:03:14.295 --> 00:03:15.405
I went to the concerts.

00:03:15.825 --> 00:03:16.605
I did all of that.

00:03:17.445 --> 00:03:18.374
I was a weeb.

00:03:18.374 --> 00:03:22.005
I don't know if you know that term
before, weebs were probably a thing.

00:03:22.214 --> 00:03:24.034
I just really loved
everything at the time.

00:03:24.064 --> 00:03:27.874
Now let's suppose that you're not 12
or 15, and you're not chasing Japanese

00:03:27.874 --> 00:03:29.674
girls and you're not into J-pop.

00:03:30.545 --> 00:03:35.674
Let's say that you're 30 or 25,
obviously something has to motivate

00:03:35.674 --> 00:03:38.614
you so it could be anime, it
could be any number of things.

00:03:38.855 --> 00:03:40.804
Well, how about the learning methodology?

00:03:40.804 --> 00:03:43.744
You know, my experience
has been that the grammar.

00:03:44.714 --> 00:03:47.954
If you just get it up front, it's very
difficult to relate it to anything until

00:03:47.954 --> 00:03:49.755
you have enough exposure to the language.

00:03:50.475 --> 00:03:51.614
I absolutely agree.

00:03:51.644 --> 00:03:52.034
Right.

00:03:52.804 --> 00:03:54.545
So this is the problem.

00:03:54.545 --> 00:03:58.385
I think, um, that I see a lot of
people, not with grammar per se.

00:03:58.734 --> 00:04:02.105
I see a lot of people when they start
learning Japanese, they immediately want

00:04:02.105 --> 00:04:07.459
to jump into learning Kanji, for example,
And it's easy to start learning kanji

00:04:07.459 --> 00:04:08.969
and then have that as your badge, right?

00:04:08.969 --> 00:04:10.410
Like I know 400 Kanji.

00:04:10.679 --> 00:04:15.269
I now know 500 Kanji, but in the end you
don't know really how to use that Kanji.

00:04:15.659 --> 00:04:18.510
You just, you're, it's, I always
like to call it a parlor trick.

00:04:18.729 --> 00:04:20.130
It's something you can brag about.

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:25.440
And I think even a lot of times classes
or a teacher, sometimes if they get a

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.659
student that goes that direction, they
let them go that direction because

00:04:27.659 --> 00:04:31.229
it's an easy way to grade them to say,
Hey, oh, look, my student made this

00:04:31.229 --> 00:04:34.950
progress, but really that's a mistake.

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:38.430
I believe that you, you know, like you
said, you can learn, you could learn,

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:42.750
you can get up to JLPT level in grammar,
the Japanese language proficiency

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:46.440
test level one, and maybe not even
know how to have great conversations.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:51.659
So what I always think is you learn
grammar, you learn a grammar point, and

00:04:51.659 --> 00:04:52.890
then you try to find a way to use it.

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:54.240
If you don't use it,
you're going to lose it.

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:58.350
And you don't want to learn above
what you are capable of doing,

00:04:58.350 --> 00:04:59.520
this is what I always believe.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:04.830
Like, uh, when, for example, when I was
learning Korean, uh, I would, I went to

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:09.360
Korea and I took a group class in the
morning and I took a private class, uh,

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:10.800
after that, or sometimes I flipped it.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:14.310
Uh, and then whatever I learned that
day, I would kind of keep in my head

00:05:14.310 --> 00:05:15.660
and that night I would go out to...

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:17.320
what they have, they have these
language meetups that you can do

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:19.045
in Korea where people get together.

00:05:19.045 --> 00:05:21.265
They're learning English and
Korean and Japanese and German.

00:05:21.265 --> 00:05:22.375
It's actually really fascinating.

00:05:23.125 --> 00:05:25.375
And I would go to those and I would
just, in my mind, I would think

00:05:25.375 --> 00:05:26.545
I'm going to use this grammar.

00:05:26.635 --> 00:05:31.705
The minute you use the grammar, you,
you build this a bigger connection in

00:05:31.705 --> 00:05:33.745
your brain and it becomes more natural.

00:05:33.895 --> 00:05:37.770
Now, you know, I don't recommend that
when you're doing something like that.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:41.610
If you learn a new grammar, never
preface it, don't say, Hey, uh, can

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:42.990
I test this new grammar with you?

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:46.830
Because then that the, the, the native
speaker is going to be on the outlook.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:52.590
What I like to do is use the grammar,
see what happens sometimes, sometimes,

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:55.860
especially in Korean, I got, you
know, I would learn a grammar, not

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:59.100
fully understand the implication of
that grammar and get a huge laugh.

00:06:00.104 --> 00:06:03.465
And now I don't know exactly
why it's funny, but now I know.

00:06:03.494 --> 00:06:03.765
Okay.

00:06:03.765 --> 00:06:05.265
There's something about
that that makes it funny.

00:06:05.414 --> 00:06:07.844
So the next time I do it again, and
I start building an understanding

00:06:07.844 --> 00:06:10.905
of why people are laughing
at that particular grammar.

00:06:10.965 --> 00:06:11.304
Right.

00:06:11.895 --> 00:06:16.695
Um, but without that experience, yeah,
you, you could blindly say things that are

00:06:16.695 --> 00:06:20.114
rude, for example, uh, I'm still gonna,
I'm just going to barrel through one more.

00:06:20.445 --> 00:06:23.145
Uh, my, uh, video editor might.

00:06:23.955 --> 00:06:25.244
He's learning Japanese.

00:06:25.275 --> 00:06:28.304
And he was saying a complicated
sentence to me the other day.

00:06:28.965 --> 00:06:32.325
And he used "nante" in
Japanese in a really weird way.

00:06:32.325 --> 00:06:40.635
And it sounded like he said something
like, uh, ... which, which could mean,

00:06:41.085 --> 00:06:44.325
you know, oh man, I can't believe
you can actually make delicious

00:06:44.325 --> 00:06:47.414
cooking, but he wasn't trying to
say that he didn't understand the

00:06:47.414 --> 00:06:49.244
implications of what "nante" does.

00:06:49.544 --> 00:06:53.730
So without using that grammar, You know,
no, the first time he uses it, he might've

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:56.730
made that mistake, but he would have
learned right away that wasn't good to do.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:59.219
So using it absolutely right
away as the best thing to do.

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:01.469
Don't use words that
you're not going to use.

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:06.090
Don't learn Kanji, you know, I'm a
big fan of just-in-time learning after

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:08.430
you get the basics down just, just
in time learning for those things.

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:09.409
Right.

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:10.159
Interesting.

00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:12.320
I mean, everyone has a different approach.

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:14.570
In my case, I had learned
Chinese beforehand.

00:07:14.570 --> 00:07:15.140
So I had that.

00:07:16.335 --> 00:07:18.794
Uh, so that was not an issue.

00:07:19.365 --> 00:07:24.945
I tend to favor as you know, a lot more
input before I speak, unless I'm there,

00:07:24.945 --> 00:07:28.094
like in your situation, you were in
Japan, you were in Korea, you have all

00:07:28.094 --> 00:07:30.555
kinds of opportunity to use the language.

00:07:30.825 --> 00:07:35.820
I guess one of the messages there is that
there's many different roads that are

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:38.250
going to take us to success in, in life.

00:07:39.299 --> 00:07:45.090
Uh, and it depends on your circumstance,
but, uh, so you, you were totally into

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:48.870
Japanese learning and then all of a
sudden you got interested in Korean.

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:50.319
Why did you get interested in Korean?

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:53.810
Could I go back just a second
though, and talk about the input.

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:57.349
So yeah, the input is
something I highly recommend.

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:02.759
Um, one of the things that, so I have the
video series on YouTube and one thing I

00:08:02.759 --> 00:08:08.009
constantly recommend to people is, is for
them to listen to Japanese radio, right.

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:11.819
Uh, find a good podcast or a
radio, but it can't be one...

00:08:11.819 --> 00:08:16.379
I firmly believe it shouldn't be one
that's designed for students of Japanese

00:08:16.379 --> 00:08:17.580
or designed for students in Korean.

00:08:18.119 --> 00:08:24.270
I want full-blown, full-speed, fully
actuated Korean or Japanese so that

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:28.830
your brain can hear things, um, that,
that you're not going to hear in a

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:32.280
classroom because it's in a sense when
you're learning in a classroom it's fun

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:33.720
and all, but it's classroom Japanese.

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:35.490
It's, it's a subset of Japanese.

00:08:35.850 --> 00:08:38.970
And you know, even me, you know,
before I went to Korea, I learned

00:08:39.299 --> 00:08:40.740
in a, with a meetup group here.

00:08:40.740 --> 00:08:43.380
So I had learned those kind of,
what's the word I'm looking for?

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:47.640
Like really clean sentences that have
no advanced grammar because it's a lower

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:50.910
level sentence, and it's not said in a
way with any "ums" or "ahs", you know?

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:54.255
But then you go to Korea and, or
you go to Japan and all of a sudden

00:08:54.255 --> 00:08:57.465
you're in a convenience store and
they say something super basic back

00:08:57.465 --> 00:08:59.325
to you, but you've never heard it.

00:08:59.595 --> 00:09:00.975
You've never been exposed to it.

00:09:01.215 --> 00:09:05.325
But if you had been watching dramas
or listening to people speak, your

00:09:05.325 --> 00:09:07.995
