00:12:06.715 --> 00:12:07.285
It's called...

00:12:08.805 --> 00:12:10.125
now you're not going to
find that one in English.

00:12:10.425 --> 00:12:11.805
It means two o'clock date.

00:12:12.135 --> 00:12:16.064
And it's, I think one of the longest
running, uh, Korean radio shows

00:12:16.185 --> 00:12:20.355
in all of Korea, uh, I listened to
it all the time and every year or

00:12:20.355 --> 00:12:21.675
so they'll change the announcer.

00:12:22.064 --> 00:12:25.005
I, it, my opinion firmly is that
the last announcer was the best,

00:12:25.395 --> 00:12:27.824
but, uh, now it's two people that...

00:12:27.824 --> 00:12:30.385
Somehow we're going to find these, I'm
going to put them in the description.

00:12:30.385 --> 00:12:32.375
I'm going to link, I'll,
I'll send you the links.

00:12:34.155 --> 00:12:37.425
Let me see if there's anything, uh,
let me see, let me just see quickly

00:12:37.425 --> 00:12:40.515
if I can easily give you something
that identifies that one easier.

00:12:41.025 --> 00:12:45.855
Uh, but yeah, that one, that one's
good because it's two people talking

00:12:45.855 --> 00:12:48.015
and they go rapid fire through topics.

00:12:48.435 --> 00:12:51.225
And then what they'll do is they'll
introduce something that like a

00:12:51.225 --> 00:12:55.145
housewife in Busan had a problem,
and then she, they, so the topics

00:12:55.145 --> 00:12:57.355
change fresh enough, right?

00:12:57.355 --> 00:13:01.075
So that if your Korean is at an
intermediate level, there might

00:13:01.075 --> 00:13:04.255
be one particular thing where all
of a sudden you understand, right?

00:13:04.345 --> 00:13:06.085
And then the next topic will come
and you might not understand.

00:13:06.085 --> 00:13:10.285
So I call that like this random
pocket of understanding and I, my

00:13:10.285 --> 00:13:13.765
Korean significantly improved just
listening to that for 30 or 40

00:13:13.765 --> 00:13:15.265
minutes, like as I was driving around.

00:13:15.805 --> 00:13:18.595
Uh, and once you get good, you start
being like, you start thinking,

00:13:19.015 --> 00:13:21.715
oh, well, I'm actually enjoying the
content you stop thinking about,

00:13:21.745 --> 00:13:22.945
oh, I'm, I'm speaking Korean.

00:13:23.820 --> 00:13:25.950
But what I get, what I get from you.

00:13:25.950 --> 00:13:30.780
And I think the big lesson is, is
your enthusiasm, your willingness

00:13:30.780 --> 00:13:34.800
to not understand your willingness
to be in a situation where you can't

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:36.060
quite say what you want to say.

00:13:36.210 --> 00:13:38.790
You don't quite understand
everything that's being said, and

00:13:38.790 --> 00:13:41.910
you're happy, nevertheless, and
you just throw yourself into it.

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:45.540
And in that, you know, that approach
is actually better than sitting in a

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:50.220
classroom, trying to nail everything down,
wanting to understand everything and never

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:52.080
getting beyond that class, you will learn.

00:13:52.875 --> 00:13:56.115
You learn so many things that the
teacher would never think to teach

00:13:56.115 --> 00:13:57.375
you by listening to people talk.

00:13:58.125 --> 00:14:02.205
And I, if you want them, uh,
Steve, I've got, I've got on here.

00:14:02.205 --> 00:14:05.715
So what I do is I get the podcast
and then I put like a, I rename

00:14:05.715 --> 00:14:06.645
it so that it goes in order.

00:14:06.645 --> 00:14:11.055
I renamed my like J uh, close
parentheses, K close parentheses.

00:14:11.415 --> 00:14:12.165
I have Vietnamese.

00:14:12.900 --> 00:14:16.740
Uh, I have Russian, whatever
I was learning for the moment.

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:17.910
Uh, I would get it.

00:14:17.910 --> 00:14:22.280
I have two or three good German
ones, so I can, I can give you a

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:23.610
good Chinese one if you want one too.

00:14:23.610 --> 00:14:25.110
How many languages are you working on?

00:14:26.474 --> 00:14:30.665
Well, I'm striving to be
the next Steve Kaufmann.

00:14:31.265 --> 00:14:31.564
Um...

00:14:31.564 --> 00:14:34.055
You've got a few more years
to go though, so that's good.

00:14:34.354 --> 00:14:38.104
No, you really inspired me because
when I heard you were 75 and you got 20

00:14:38.104 --> 00:14:41.224
languages, I thought, well, I'm 48, right?

00:14:41.344 --> 00:14:43.805
I've got Japanese, Korean.

00:14:44.285 --> 00:14:46.324
I'm fairly happy with my Chinese.

00:14:46.324 --> 00:14:47.645
Although I do need more work.

00:14:48.035 --> 00:14:48.964
I started German.

00:14:49.444 --> 00:14:52.859
Uh, and then once, um, What's his name?

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:53.459
Oh my gosh.

00:14:53.640 --> 00:14:58.199
I shouldn't forget this
Laoshu50500, when he passed away.

00:14:58.259 --> 00:14:58.349
Right.

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:02.490
Uh, I had, I had, uh, I had only a
brief interaction with him and it wasn't

00:15:02.490 --> 00:15:05.640
a positive one, unfortunately, but
when he passed away, I went up to his

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:07.079
channel and I watched more of his stuff.

00:15:07.079 --> 00:15:09.960
I found out how young he was and
I saw on his Facebook, he's doing

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:13.020
these really weird languages that
I would never think to learn.

00:15:13.079 --> 00:15:13.439
Right.

00:15:13.829 --> 00:15:18.030
And then I thought that guy had,
he had inspired so many people.

00:15:18.864 --> 00:15:19.824
I thought, what am I doing?

00:15:19.824 --> 00:15:21.685
I'm just sitting here not
learning more languages.

00:15:21.685 --> 00:15:25.435
So that day I booked Tagalog,
which I've really loved.

00:15:25.505 --> 00:15:26.785
Tagalog is amazing.

00:15:27.234 --> 00:15:29.124
And, uh, and Vietnamese.

00:15:30.015 --> 00:15:30.435
So...

00:15:31.035 --> 00:15:36.015
I mean, Laushu was amazing, very
humble guy interested in, you

00:15:36.015 --> 00:15:41.145
know, it didn't Hmong, uh, you
know, no Georgian, you name it.

00:15:41.145 --> 00:15:45.194
The guy was, and he had such a breadth
of interests in terms of languages.

00:15:45.525 --> 00:15:47.404
And another guy who was doing
that is Richard Simcott.

00:15:47.425 --> 00:15:52.994
I just saw him speaking, uh, Estonian and,
and people, I think, you know, we don't

00:15:52.994 --> 00:15:54.494
have to be perfect in these languages.

00:15:54.494 --> 00:15:58.005
It's, it's such a delight to discover a
new language, to explore a new language.

00:15:58.694 --> 00:15:59.834
Uh, so good for you.

00:16:00.255 --> 00:16:01.305
Absolutely good for you.

00:16:01.535 --> 00:16:02.285
It really is.

00:16:02.285 --> 00:16:06.995
You know, th there's, there's a, there's
a subset in the Japanese community.

00:16:07.135 --> 00:16:10.925
I haven't seen it in the Korean community
yet where they're striving for perfection

00:16:11.495 --> 00:16:15.155
or they, they put a lot of emphasis
on the Japanese learning community

00:16:15.905 --> 00:16:17.375
in the Japanese learning community.

00:16:17.885 --> 00:16:20.880
Um, It's never been my thing.

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:25.560
You know, I saw a TED talk once and it,
it really summed up everything I've ever

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:31.620
believed about learning a language is that
language is a tool, not an art to master.

00:16:31.680 --> 00:16:34.340
And it's a tool for communicating.

00:16:34.460 --> 00:16:37.790
It's also a tool for discovering more
about different cultures, different

00:16:37.790 --> 00:16:38.870
countries and stuff like that.

00:16:39.080 --> 00:16:42.680
It's not, it's not a, it's not
synchronized diving at the Olympic games.

00:16:44.545 --> 00:16:44.935
Right.

00:16:45.605 --> 00:16:46.905
We don't have to achieve perfection.

00:16:46.935 --> 00:16:48.805
If that's what people want
to do, that's fine too.

00:16:48.805 --> 00:16:48.955
Yeah.

00:16:50.505 --> 00:16:54.075
Well, that's the thing that it really
is, especially nowadays, if you want

00:16:54.075 --> 00:16:58.155
to strive for perfection, you can, but
my question is what's your goal, right?

00:16:58.575 --> 00:17:02.295
Is that your goal or goal is
to make friends in the country?

00:17:02.325 --> 00:17:03.885
Always a hundred percent.

00:17:03.915 --> 00:17:08.204
So my goal in a new language is to
get two friends that don't speak

00:17:08.204 --> 00:17:12.305
English so that there's no influence
of the western mind on them.

00:17:12.700 --> 00:17:17.220
But one more thing though, is this
by learning, say Tagalog or Korean or

00:17:17.220 --> 00:17:21.540
whatever, you're learning, you have a
different perception of those people.

00:17:21.900 --> 00:17:23.250
Like those people come alive.

00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:27.510
All of a sudden, that's just not
a place on the globe or a map.

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:29.160
Those are real people.

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:33.060
Now you have a bit of a sense,
not maybe very deep yet, but a

00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:34.410
bit of a sense of those people.

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:39.270
That's much more alive than
when you have never even spoken

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:40.290
or understood any of them.

00:17:41.325 --> 00:17:43.725
Well, if I can add to that
and say something slightly

00:17:43.725 --> 00:17:48.315
controversial, if you're living in
America, that's where I live now.

00:17:48.315 --> 00:17:52.395
So I see this, you know, we don't
really understand foreigners because

00:17:52.395 --> 00:17:55.635
we're America and we're number
one and everyone speaks English.

00:17:55.635 --> 00:17:56.055
Right, right.

00:17:56.265 --> 00:18:02.085
So we're used to non native speaking
English and maybe not a perfect way.

00:18:02.115 --> 00:18:02.265
Right.

00:18:03.165 --> 00:18:06.645
So then maybe we think, oh, they're not so
smart, but the minute you start speaking

00:18:06.645 --> 00:18:11.515
in their language, you go, you realize
they are really smart, just like we are.

00:18:12.235 --> 00:18:14.085
And it's a controversial thing
because that's a weird thing to

00:18:14.085 --> 00:18:15.615
say, but I'll give you an example.

00:18:16.485 --> 00:18:20.455
I made a friend in Korea through
Hello Talk, which is an amazing

00:18:20.455 --> 00:18:21.505
app that I absolutely love.

00:18:22.075 --> 00:18:25.615
And, uh, when I first met her, I
just thought she was kinda dumb.

00:18:25.615 --> 00:18:29.665
Like just not super intelligent, but
it was my Korean level that needed

00:18:29.665 --> 00:18:32.985
to increase because once I spoke more
Korean, I found out she was deep-thinking,

00:18:33.355 --> 00:18:34.435
you know, she had deep thought.

00:18:34.445 --> 00:18:35.605
She was just a quiet person.

00:18:36.115 --> 00:18:36.685
You know, I...

00:18:37.175 --> 00:18:38.185
This is the problem.

00:18:38.185 --> 00:18:39.535
This is why people are intimidated.

00:18:40.385 --> 00:18:43.145
Uh, about speaking another language
because they consider themselves

00:18:43.145 --> 00:18:44.695
intelligent in their own language.

00:18:44.715 --> 00:18:47.795
Doesn't matter who you are,
American, Chinese, whatever.

00:18:48.245 --> 00:18:50.525
And you realize that when you're
speaking this foreign language,

00:18:50.585 --> 00:18:51.485
all of a sudden you're lessened.

00:18:52.335 --> 00:18:54.855
So most adults don't like that.

00:18:54.855 --> 00:18:58.005
Like kids don't care, kids just
want to, you know, play with their

00:18:58.005 --> 00:19:01.275
friends, but an adult thinks, well,
you know, I'm an educated person

00:19:01.275 --> 00:19:03.105
in my language, whatever it may be.

00:19:03.405 --> 00:19:06.435
And now I'm going to speak this other
language and all of a sudden I'm less,

00:19:06.705 --> 00:19:08.625
or at least come across as less educated.

00:19:09.135 --> 00:19:12.315
That never bothered me but there
are people who are bothered by that.

00:19:12.315 --> 00:19:15.075
And I think we have to
get beyond that and.

00:19:15.990 --> 00:19:19.560
Just strive to communicate and,
and not worry about how, you

00:19:19.560 --> 00:19:23.370
know, whatever imperfections there
are and how we use the language.

00:19:24.690 --> 00:19:26.820
I mean, we all like to improve.

00:19:26.850 --> 00:19:27.290
That's fine.

00:19:27.640 --> 00:19:32.150
It's extremely frustrating when you're
learning a new language, because you

00:19:32.150 --> 00:19:34.910
know how to say things in English.

00:19:35.540 --> 00:19:38.650
And even for me, you know, I already
knew how to say things in Japanese.

00:19:38.950 --> 00:19:41.320
Now I can't say them in Korean
or I can't say them in Chinese.

00:19:41.680 --> 00:19:42.765
So you get very frustrated.

00:19:42.765 --> 00:19:45.705
And this is a common thing that
I see over these 20 plus years of

00:19:45.705 --> 00:19:47.475
teaching Japanese and now Korean.

00:19:47.775 --> 00:19:49.425
So I give this advice all the time.

00:19:49.425 --> 00:19:52.815
I say, say what you can say,
not what you want to say.

00:19:54.555 --> 00:19:58.385
I don't find it frustrating when I kind
of say things because I then reflect

00:19:58.385 --> 00:19:59.855
back on where I was six months ago.

00:20:01.665 --> 00:20:04.515
Well, you're also a veteran, you're
a veteran, uh, language learner.

00:20:04.525 --> 00:20:06.045
So you're aware and I'm aware of that too.

00:20:06.105 --> 00:20:09.785
So I say look at me all of a sudden I
can say things in Persian or Arabic.

00:20:10.235 --> 00:20:12.875
I can't quite nail it
the way I would like to.

00:20:12.965 --> 00:20:15.005
Uh, I keep on forgetting
things that I know.

00:20:15.005 --> 00:20:19.565
I know these words and I can't find them
when I'm speaking, but so what, you know,

00:20:19.595 --> 00:20:21.425
look at me now versus six months earlier.

00:20:22.295 --> 00:20:24.725
So we have to give ourselves
credit for what we've done.

00:20:26.005 --> 00:20:27.775
Yeah, you, you have to
recognize where you are.

00:20:27.775 --> 00:20:31.435
And I think, you know, we're like
that frog in a boiling pot that

00:20:31.435 --> 00:20:32.935
we don't realize how hot it got.

00:20:33.025 --> 00:20:33.235
Right.

00:20:33.235 --> 00:20:36.535
But in a better way, we don't
know how good we've got until we

00:20:36.535 --> 00:20:38.655
compare ourselves to someone else
or we start another language.

00:20:38.655 --> 00:20:39.975
But why don't we end it here?

00:20:40.665 --> 00:20:40.875
Okay.

00:20:40.905 --> 00:20:41.355
Okay.

00:20:41.775 --> 00:20:45.705
And, uh, the main thing that you
exude is enthusiasm, plus a lot

00:20:45.705 --> 00:20:48.495
of experience in learning these
languages and helping others learn.

00:20:48.495 --> 00:20:50.205
So thank you very much
for the conversation.

00:20:51.105 --> 00:20:51.405
All right.

00:20:51.405 --> 00:20:51.975
Hey, thanks.

00:20:52.245 --> 00:20:52.905
See you next time.

00:20:52.935 --> 00:20:53.235
Okay.

00:20:53.265 --> 00:20:53.895
Have a good day, Steve.

00:20:54.255 --> 00:20:54.735
Thank you.

